TESTDATA["pet7-t4"] = {"title":"Cambridge PET 7 · Test 4","format":"2010","reading":[{"part":1,"items":[{"n":1,"text":"End of Term Party\nDecember 15th 7–10 pm\nSign below if you can come!\nWe also need people to help organise the event – call Sophie (678853)","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["You need to phone Sophie if you want to come to this event.","Anyone wanting to take part in running this event should sign the notice.","People who can attend this event should put their names on the notice."],"answer":"C"},{"n":2,"text":"The Guitar Show returns to Hinton. Lots of current and brand new guitars will be on display. The show is open to schools and the public.","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"The main purpose of this email is","options":["to invite people to display goods at an exhibition.","to explain booking details for a show.","to provide information about an event."],"answer":"C"},{"n":3,"text":"Hi Abdul,\nI won’t be in college as I’m not well. Please call round on your way in to pick up my homework – it’s due in today. Thanks, Aziz","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"Aziz wants Abdul to","options":["take his homework to college for him.","call their college to say that he is not well.","pick up any new homework given out at college today."],"answer":"A"},{"n":4,"text":"SOUP: Once opened, remove any unused soup from the tin and place in the refrigerator.","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"This label gives advice on","options":["how to store the product.","how to use the product.","how to open the product."],"answer":"A"},{"n":5,"text":"Wanted: Kitchen Assistants\nEvenings or weekends\nFree meals\nFull training provided\nApply inside","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Only people who are trained in kitchen work should apply for these part-time jobs.","There are part-time opportunities for people without experience of working in a kitchen.","We offer cheap meals to people who work part-time in our kitchen."],"answer":"B"}]},{"part":2,"people":[{"n":6,"name":"Jake and Maureen","text":"Jake and Maureen have different interests but want to go somewhere they will both enjoy. Jake loves anything to do with the sea, while Maureen’s interested in social history and the lives of women.","answer":"F"},{"n":7,"name":"Melanie","text":"Melanie is a history teacher. She wants to take her class to a museum where they will be allowed to touch things and have activities to do during their visit.","answer":"C"},{"n":8,"name":"Roger","text":"Roger is keen to find out about different forms of transport in towns over the last hundred years. He would like to attend a talk while he is at the museum.","answer":"H"},{"n":9,"name":"Kazuko","text":"Kazuko studies English literature and thinks that seeing where writers lived will help her understand their books better. She finds it useful to look at pictures which are connected with the books.","answer":"D"},{"n":10,"name":"Nick","text":"Nick is more interested in learning about the countryside than learning about people from the past. He needs information for some schoolwork he is doing about what happens to rivers and hills over time.","answer":"B"}],"texts":[{"letter":"A","title":"Red House","text":"Travel back to the time of the novelist Charlotte Brontë and find out about her friends and local connections. Discover the comforts and discomforts of the 1830s country home where Charlotte sometimes stayed as a guest of Joshua Taylor’s family and got ideas for her novel Shirley."},{"letter":"B","title":"Castle Museum","text":"The castle was built looking down on the valley of the River Dean. It is now a museum, where you can learn how the valley has changed over millions of years. There is a collection of interesting rocks and fossils found in the area, and pictures showing how the valley probably looked in prehistoric times."},{"letter":"C","title":"Hillcrest Museum","text":"Enjoy an experience of digging up the past. Handle pieces of ancient pots, tools and other objects from many countries. Learn to date them and see what they tell us about how people lived then. Also try out computer programs which help plan the digging-up of sites. School groups should book in advance."},{"letter":"D","title":"Shandy Hall","text":"Here in the 1760s, Laurence Sterne wrote Tristram Shandy. His fifteenth-century house is now surrounded by a large garden full of beautiful and unusual plants. Inside there is an important collection of Sterne’s novels plus the original drawings which were included in his works."},{"letter":"E","title":"Museum of Peace","text":"Situated in beautiful countryside, this is the only museum of its kind in the country. It has a growing collection of art and objects connected with peace history, non-violence and the ending of war. It also has material which can be used after the visit back in the classroom."},{"letter":"F","title":"Fishing Centre","text":"Study the routes used by fishermen over the last two hundred years and see how their ocean-going boats worked. A new exhibition explores the position of wives and mothers in the old fishing towns, giving information about their occupations, and their attitudes to work and home."},{"letter":"G","title":"Viking Centre","text":"Come and experience what life was like 1,200 years ago in this Viking town, rebuilt at the mouth of the river. Our introductory film will inform you about the close relationship the Vikings had with the sea – they were excellent fishermen, sailors and boat-builders. There are also tools, clothes and everyday items to look at."},{"letter":"H","title":"Horsepower Museum","text":"This museum reminds us just how important horses were to life in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. It has a permanent exhibition of old trams and buses, which used to be pulled through the streets by horses. The staff here are very knowledgeable and give daily lectures on this fascinating collection."}]},{"part":3,"title":"John Chapman","text":"The atmosphere in the market place in Norwich in 1440 was probably not very different from how it is today – noisy, crowded, colourful and exciting. It was here that John Chapman used to come each week from his home in Swaffham, 50 kilometres away, to try to sell his copper pots and pans.\n\nAfter one particularly tiring day, he loaded his unsold pots and pans onto the back of his horse as usual and walked slowly home. He had a meal and went to bed, complaining bitterly to his wife about their lack of money. However, that night John had a dream that would change the rest of his life.\n\nIn this dream a man told John that if he stood on London Bridge he would hear something that would make him rich. The dream was so real that John couldn’t get it out of his mind, and finally he decided to make the journey, even though his wife was against the idea.\n\nAfter a week’s preparation, John set off for London with just his dog for company. When he arrived at London Bridge he stopped and watched all the men and women who went past. Many of them talked to him, but he heard nothing that would make him rich.\n\nOn the third day, however, an old man asked him why he was standing there. John told him it was because of a dream. The old man replied, ‘I recently dreamed that I went to the home of John Chapman, in Swaffham, and dug under a tree at the back of his house, where I found a buried pot of gold! But I am not foolish enough to believe in dreams.’\n\nUnable to believe his luck, John said goodbye and returned to Swaffham. As soon as he got home, he fetched a spade and started digging. His wife looked on in amazement, unable to understand what he was doing. But sure enough, he uncovered a box. Opening it with nervous hands he found that it was full of money. The couple were delighted, but also curious about some words on the lid, which were in a language they didn’t recognise. Keen to find out their meaning, John put the box in his window and soon two young men knocked on the door and translated them for him: Beneath me lies another one much richer. So John dug deeper and this time found a huge pot full of gold and jewels!\n\nThat is how John Chapman became rich. He spent the money wisely and paid for several public buildings to be built. And his memory lives on in Swaffham today, on the painted sign at the entrance to the town!","items":[{"n":11,"q":"In 1440, John Chapman lived in Norwich.","answer":"B"},{"n":12,"q":"John rarely managed to sell all his goods at the market.","answer":"A"},{"n":13,"q":"John and his wife had enough money to live comfortably.","answer":"B"},{"n":14,"q":"John’s wife encouraged him to make the journey to London.","answer":"B"},{"n":15,"q":"People stopped to chat to John while he was standing on London Bridge.","answer":"A"},{"n":16,"q":"John was disappointed by what the old man told him.","answer":"B"},{"n":17,"q":"John’s wife realised why he was digging a hole under the tree.","answer":"B"},{"n":18,"q":"The writing on the box was in a language that was foreign to John.","answer":"A"},{"n":19,"q":"The second container was buried directly under the first.","answer":"A"},{"n":20,"q":"The people of Swaffham benefitted from John’s good luck.","answer":"A"}]},{"part":4,"title":"Anna Gomez","text":"Anna Gomez is a successful TV sports presenter, but most people still think of her as the famous women’s ice-skating champion who won several important competitions when she was younger. ‘As a child, my dream was actually to become a ballet dancer – I didn’t own any skates until I was nine, and didn’t become really keen on skating until I was fourteen,’ says Anna. She went on to university where, despite ice-skating almost all the time, she left with an excellent degree. ‘I felt I owed it to my parents. They supported me through university, and expected me to do well there – I didn’t feel I could disappoint them.’\n\nIn a sport where most stars become famous as teenagers, Anna was unusual in being in her twenties before she won any major titles. ‘When I was younger, I got very angry at competitions, shouting at judges if I disagreed with them. That made me unpopular, especially with other skaters. My technique was just as good as theirs and I had a very encouraging coach at the time. The problem was in my head – I just didn’t think I had what it takes to be a champion. That changed as I won more competitions, and I was performing at my best by the age of twenty-five.’\n\nAnna retired from professional ice-skating five years later, having achieved great success during that period. ‘It was a difficult decision. As you get near the end of your career, people always ask when you’re going to stop. At the time, I wasn’t sure that I was actually ready to give up. But, looking back, I’m glad I stopped when I did. You shouldn’t think too much about the past – just move on to the next thing.’","items":[{"n":21,"q":"What is the writer doing in the text?","options":["discussing the influence of Anna’s early life on her career","giving Anna’s reasons for choosing to become a TV presenter","describing the progress of Anna’s professional life in sport","explaining why Anna was such a successful ice-skater"],"answer":"C"},{"n":22,"q":"What do we learn about Anna’s time at university?","options":["Her parents expected her to do better in her studies.","She spent too much time ice-skating.","She managed to get good results.","Her parents wanted her to keep up her ice-skating."],"answer":"C"},{"n":23,"q":"Why does Anna believe she failed to win competitions as a teenager?","options":["She annoyed too many people.","She was not confident enough in her ability.","She needed to improve her technique.","She did not have the right coach."],"answer":"B"},{"n":24,"q":"What does Anna say about her retirement from skating?","options":["She has no regrets about giving up when she did.","She had wanted to stop for a long time.","She was persuaded by others that she should retire.","She missed some things about her life as an ice-skater."],"answer":"A"},{"n":25,"q":"Which of the following appeared on a website about Anna?","options":["Anna Gomez, 20, achieves her life-long ambition of becoming the national women’s ice-skating champion.","TV presenter and former sportswoman Anna Gomez announces her return to professional ice-skating after five years.","Teenage star Anna Gomez wins another top title, and thanks her many friends in the ice-skating world.","After five years at the top, ice-skating star Anna Gomez retires and takes up a new job in television."],"answer":"D"}]},{"part":5,"title":"The Driest Place on Earth","text":"The Atacama Desert in Chile is (0) ……… (known) as the driest place on Earth. It is almost 1,000 kilometres [26] length, lying between the Pacific Ocean and the Andes mountains. Under a centimetre of rain [27] annually, and the centre is so dry [28] scientists have never recorded [29] rain there.\nOver a million people [30] live in the Atacama today. Most live on the coast, which is also home to teams of astronomers who are there to [31] advantage of the clear skies. In the north, farmers grow tomatoes with water they have collected from underground rocks. However, for [32] who have their farms on higher ground, the water comes from melting snow.\nPeople generally seem to [33] knowledge about what the desert has to offer, but in [34], there is plenty to do – from seeing the amazing natural sights to playing golf, one of the more recent activities [35] tourists to the region.","gaps":{"26":{"options":["by","at","to","in"],"answer":"D"},"27":{"options":["pours","falls","sinks","lowers"],"answer":"B"},"28":{"options":["as","that","when","than"],"answer":"B"},"29":{"options":["any","no","some","little"],"answer":"A"},"30":{"options":["totally","exactly","actually","finally"],"answer":"C"},"31":{"options":["have","make","get","take"],"answer":"D"},"32":{"options":["those","these","them","they"],"answer":"A"},"33":{"options":["fail","lose","miss","lack"],"answer":"D"},"34":{"options":["case","turn","fact","time"],"answer":"C"},"35":{"options":["arriving","attracting","approaching","arranging"],"answer":"B"}}}],"writing":[{"part":1,"title":"Here are some sentences about a trip to the city of Florence in Italy. Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first, using no more than three words.","items":[{"n":1,"first":"Florence is not as crowded in winter as it is in summer.","before":"Florence is","after":"crowded in winter than in summer.","answers":["less"]},{"n":2,"first":"John suggested taking a taxi to the hotel.","before":"John said, ‘If I were you,","after":"take a taxi to the hotel.’","answers":["I would","I'd"]},{"n":3,"first":"Florence is a very easy city to walk around.","before":"It’s not very","after":"to walk around Florence.","answers":["difficult at all","difficult","hard at all","hard"]},{"n":4,"first":"A city like Florence can teach you a lot.","before":"You can","after":"a lot in a city like Florence.","answers":["learn","find out","discover","teach yourself","be taught"]},{"n":5,"first":"I will probably visit some museums while I’m in Florence.","before":"I will probably visit some museums","after":"my stay in Florence.","answers":["during"]}]},{"part":2,"items":[{"n":6,"kind":"message","task":"You have just bought some new clothes.\n\nWrite an email to your friend, Alex. In your email, you should\n• tell Alex what clothes you have bought\n• say where you bought the clothes from\n• explain why you needed to buy these clothes.\n\nWrite 35–45 words on your answer sheet.","points":["dire quali vestiti nuovi ha comprato","dire dove li ha comprati","spiegare perché gli/le servivano"]}]},{"part":3,"items":[{"n":7,"kind":"letter","task":"This is part of a letter you receive from your English penfriend.\n\n‘I live in a really busy street. What is it like where you live? If you were able to move, where would you like to live?’\n\nNow write a letter, answering your penfriend’s questions.\nWrite your letter in about 100 words on your answer sheet."},{"n":8,"kind":"story","task":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story.\nThis is the title for your story:\n\nAn unusual request\n\nWrite your story in about 100 words on your answer sheet."}]}],"listening":[{"part":1,"audio":"audio/pet7-t4-l1.mp3","items":[{"n":1,"q":"Which prize has the man just won?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"q":"What was the man’s first job?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":3,"q":"Where will they have something to eat?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":4,"q":"What does the woman’s house look like now?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":5,"q":"Which sport will they do tomorrow?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":6,"q":"What can you see on the television programme?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":7,"q":"Where will the man sit on the plane?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"}],"example":"img/pet7-t4-l1-ex.jpg"},{"part":2,"intro":"You will hear an interview with a woman called Lucy Rainbow, who is talking about her job as a painter.","audio":"audio/pet7-t4-l2.mp3","items":[{"n":8,"q":"What does Lucy usually paint?","options":["scenery for stage plays","pictures of pop stars","the walls in people’s homes"],"answer":"A"},{"n":9,"q":"Lucy chose her present job because","options":["she enjoys working by herself.","she couldn’t get a job in advertising.","she thought it would be interesting."],"answer":"C"},{"n":10,"q":"What does Lucy find difficult about her work?","options":["She sometimes misses lunch.","Some days are too busy.","She always has too much work to do."],"answer":"B"},{"n":11,"q":"How many hours a day does Lucy usually work?","options":["seven","eight","eleven"],"answer":"C"},{"n":12,"q":"How does Lucy travel to work?","options":["on foot","by car","by public transport"],"answer":"C"},{"n":13,"q":"What does Lucy do in her free time nowadays?","options":["She studies.","She visits an art gallery.","She plays tennis."],"answer":"A"}]},{"part":3,"intro":"You will hear a radio announcement about a new magazine.","audio":"audio/pet7-t4-l3.mp3","notes":"NEW MAGAZINE\n\nThe name of the magazine is [14].\n\nFirst issue of magazine\n• healthy recipes using [15]\n• a DVD about making [16] for summer\n\nSecond issue of magazine\n• recipes using [17] for children\n• special recipes to use for [18] for adults\n\nThe first issue of the magazine costs £ [19]","answers":{"14":["Good Living"],"15":["fish","fishes"],"16":["salads","salad"],"17":["rices","rice"],"18":["parties","party"],"19":["£2.49","2.49","two pounds and forty nine","two pounds forty nine","two pound and forty nine","two pound forty nine","two pounds and forty-nine","two pounds forty-nine","two pound and forty-nine","two pound forty-nine","two pounds and forty nine p","two pounds forty nine p","two pound and forty nine p","two pound forty nine p","two pounds and forty-nine pence","two pounds forty-nine pence","two pound and forty-nine pence","two pound forty-nine pence","two pounds and forty nine pence","two pounds forty nine pence","two pound and forty nine pence","two pound forty nine pence"]}},{"part":4,"intro":"You will hear a man called Karl, and his wife Jenny, talking about the holiday they have just had.","audio":"audio/pet7-t4-l4.mp3","items":[{"n":20,"q":"Jenny and Karl are both pleased to be home after their holiday.","answer":"B"},{"n":21,"q":"Jenny thinks the weather forecast they heard for their holiday week was correct.","answer":"A"},{"n":22,"q":"Jenny and Karl both liked the way their hotel served meals.","answer":"A"},{"n":23,"q":"Jenny thinks they had a better room on this holiday than last year.","answer":"B"},{"n":24,"q":"Karl was angry about the state of the hotel sports equipment.","answer":"B"},{"n":25,"q":"Jenny and Karl are both keen to plan another holiday immediately.","answer":"B"}]}]};
